   "text": "My search is complete. Final specification: **[feature 38 long, feature 8 long, feature 27 long]**, equal-weight, in-sample annualized Sharpe = 0.7627.\n\nNow for the out-of-sample prediction. Key considerations:\n- In-sample Sharpe (0.76) was built via greedy search over 40 features with 3 content moves \u2014 there's real selection/overfitting risk even with only 3 moves, since each move picked the best among ~76-80 candidates.\n- The diminishing marginal gains (0.70 \u2192 0.75 \u2192 0.76) suggest the combination is converging to genuine signal rather than being pure noise-chasing, since pure overfitting typically keeps finding large \"improvements\" chasing noise.\n- Costs are already net (5bps turnover, 50bps borrow, 1-day lag), which is a realistic, conservative setting \u2014 the in-sample number already reflects real-world frictions, somewhat limiting how much of it is a costless-backtest mirage.\n- Still, with 40 instruments and 40 features and a greedy multi-step search, I should expect meaningful out-of-sample shrinkage \u2014 typically to roughly half to two-thirds of in-sample for a search this shallow and disciplined.\n\nI'll predict a mean OOS Sharpe around 0.40\u20130.45 with a standard deviation reflecting substantial uncertainty (~0.35), given the small number of search steps limits overfitting but real decay is still expected."
